🚀 8 Types of Classes in C# Every .NET Developer Should Know

Not all classes in C# are designed for the same purpose.

Understanding different class types helps you write cleaner, more maintainable, reusable, and well-structured .NET applications.

Here are 8 important types of classes every C# developer should know:

1️⃣ Regular Class
The standard class used to define objects with properties, methods, and constructors.

2️⃣ Static Class
Cannot be instantiated or inherited. Contains only static members. Ideal for utility and helper methods.

3️⃣ Abstract Class
Cannot be instantiated directly. Provides a common base for derived classes and supports shared implementation.

4️⃣ Sealed Class
Prevents other classes from inheriting from it. Useful when inheritance should be restricted.

5️⃣ Partial Class
Allows a class definition to be split across multiple files. Commonly used in generated code and large applications.

6️⃣ Nested Class
A class declared inside another class. Useful for encapsulating closely related functionality.

7️⃣ Generic Class
Uses type parameters to create reusable, type-safe components.

8️⃣ Record Class
Designed primarily for data models, with value-based equality by default.

💡 A few important things to remember:
🔹static controls instantiation and inheritance.
🔹abstract supports abstraction and shared base behavior.
🔹sealed prevents inheritance.
🔹partial allows splitting a class definition.
🔹record provides value-based equality.
🔹generic enables type-safe reusability.

These features can also be combined where C# allows it.

